    <DataNode elementId="AT3G55030-MONOMER" textLabel="CDP-diacylglycerol--glycerol-3-phosphate 3-phosphatidyltransferase 2" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT3G55030-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="555" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">In plant cells, phosphatidylglycerolphosphate synthase (PGPS) is located in mitochondria, plastids, and ER. Two PGPS have been characterized from Arabidopsis, PGPS1 and PGPS2. PGPS1 is dual-targeted to mitochondria and plastids|CITS: [12609031]|, whereas PGPS2 is mainly found in the microsome fraction. Nonetheless, the two isozymes have similar properties. Both have a pH optimum of 8.5, a requirement for divalent cation, and very similar Kms for substrates in the micromolar range. Triton X-100 stimulates enzyme activity for both isozymes. The two isozymes are more active with CDP-dipalmitoylglycerol than with CDP-dioleoylglycerol.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G55030-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="Phosphatidylglycerophosphate synthase 2" />
